  "note": [
   {
    "_key": "note_1",
    "book": "@book_1",
    "member": "@member_2",
    "content": "Loved how Adiga portrays the duality of India - the Light and the Darkness. Balram's journey from servant to entrepreneur is both disturbing and compelling. The rooster coop metaphor stayed with me.",
    "meeting": "@meeting_1",
    "created_at": "2024-02-10T20:15:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-02-10T20:15:00Z"
   },
   {
    "_key": "note_2",
    "book": "@book_1",
    "member": "@member_3",
    "content": "The epistolary format (letter to Wen Jiabao) was an interesting choice. Made me question the reliability of the narrator. Great discussion on whether Balram's actions were justified.",
    "meeting": "@meeting_1",
    "created_at": "2024-02-10T20:20:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-02-10T20:20:00Z"
   },
   {
    "_key": "note_3",
    "book": "@book_3",
    "member": "@member_5",
    "content": "Reading this for the second time - noticing more details about Swedish corporate culture and the Vanger family dynamics. Lisbeth Salander remains one of the most complex female protagonists.",
    "meeting": null,
    "created_at": "2024-03-05T14:30:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-03-05T14:30:00Z"
   },
   {
    "_key": "note_4",
    "book": "@book_5",
    "member": "@member_7",
    "content": "Austen's wit shines through every page. Elizabeth Bennet's refusal of Mr. Collins was revolutionary for its time. The social commentary on entailment and women's limited options is still relevant.",
    "meeting": null,
    "created_at": "2024-03-22T11:00:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-03-22T11:00:00Z"
   }
  ],
